{"id": 32, "ts": 1781199035.287723, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 2, "agent": "emily_brooks", "message": "I remain convinced that Candidate\u202fC is the strongest overall choice.\u202fBuilding on Sarah\u2019s and James\u2019s points, C\u2019s **100\u202f% reliability**, **rapid correct decision\u2011making**, **calmness in crises**, **understanding of complex technology**, and **excellent attention** directly satisfy the core safety criteria for long\u2011distance operations.\u202fIn contrast, the recent evidence on the other candidates underscores why they fall short:\u202fCandidate\u202fB, while very conscientious, exhibits **pretentiousness and nasty remarks** that pose a higher risk to crew cooperation than C\u2019s communication difficulty; Candidate\u202fD shows **adequate stress handling** but his **hot temper, know\u2011it\u2011all attitude, and loner reputation** raise serious concerns about crew collaboration and composure under pressure; Candidate\u202fA lacks any concrete data on reliability, stress resilience, or teamwork and also has **unfriendly behavior**.\u202fThus, despite C\u2019s modest adaptability gap (limited willingness to pursue further education), his safety\u2011critical strengths outweigh the interpersonal concerns, which can be mitigated through targeted crew\u2011resource\u2011training and mentorship.\u202fAccordingly, I vote for **Candidate\u202fC**."}
